  "question": "Should path-stable snapshot indexes keep mutating one shared provenance record when authored accepted and projected history can branch from the same source snapshot?",
  "model": "GPT-6 (Codex)",
  "method": "best-api-review/history-v2",
  "trigger": "Patch failed-fix recovery reproduced an authored undo crash after a heading reset and two word deletions; the UI repair exposed shared mutable projection-index provenance in Plite.",
  "requirements": [
    "Undo and redo may advance accepted and projected documents independently from one source snapshot without corrupting either branch.",
    "Ordinary linear path-stable commits retain snapshot-index identity and avoid document-wide reconstruction.",
    "Structural mappings reject a different source snapshot even when its token length happens to match.",
    "The invariant belongs to Plite snapshot indexing; Plate deletion rules and authored callers must not coordinate around it."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "The 2026-09-11 reads review established lazy snapshot indexes and demand-driven identity lookup as the current owner. Current snapshot-index, projection inheritance, authored finish, history replay, and the AI docs consumer were inspected; the reporter interaction was freshly replayed against the working tree.",
  "summary": "Pursue copy-on-write branch recovery in the existing Plite snapshot index: keep in-place provenance for the linear path-stable hot path, and lazily rebuild from the exact source snapshot only when a shared index branches.",
  "verdict": "pursue",
  "previous": "2026-09-11-reads-demand-driven-invalidation",
  "relation": "reaffirms",
  "alternatives": [
    "Keep/configure: rejected because shared in-place provenance crashes a valid authored undo branch.",
    "Patch authored history or Plate heading deletion: rejected because callers cannot safely coordinate snapshot-index ownership and the same branch can occur outside heading deletion.",
    "Always allocate a persistent index per path-stable commit: rejected because existing core contracts require stable index identity for cached linear text edits.",
    "Always remap through the full structural mapper: rejected because path-stable edits do not move identities and the extra work discards the large-document optimization.",
    "Copy on branch at the existing snapshot-index owner: selected; linear edits keep their identity, while a detected older source receives a fresh lazy index from that exact document.",
    "Replace snapshot indexing: rejected because the current lazy identity and compact structural mapping contracts remain useful once branch ownership is corrected."
  ],
